# I lost my password (or my 2FA)

You forgot your password, lost your phone with the authenticator
app, or lost the hardware key. None of this is unrecoverable —
this page tells you which path matches your situation.

## Case 1: I forgot my password (email still works)

Use Keycloak's self-service password reset.

1. Go to [{{ keycloak_hostname }}](https://{{ keycloak_hostname }}).
2. Click **Sign In** under whichever realm matches your account.
3. On the login form, click **Forgot Password?**.
4. Type the email address tied to your account.
5. Check your inbox — Keycloak sends a one-time link. Click it,
   set a new password.

If the email never arrives, check the spam folder and the
"updates" / "promotions" tab. Some providers route automated mail
oddly.

If after 10 minutes there is still nothing, contact your operator.

## Case 2: I forgot my password AND my email is unreachable

Self-service won't work. Contact your operator with:

- Your account name (the email address you signed in with).
- Confirmation of your identity over a channel you both already
  trust (a phone call, a video call, an in-person handover) — not
  email, because we just established email is the problem.

Your operator sets a one-time password on your account that you
change at next sign-in. They will send it over the channel you
identified yourselves on.

## Case 3: I lost my 2FA (phone wiped, hardware key gone)

Your password still works; only the second-factor device is
missing.

1. Try to sign in normally — Keycloak gets you to the password
   step, then asks for the 2FA code.
2. If your authenticator backup (cloud sync, written-down recovery
   codes) is reachable, use that.
3. Otherwise: contact your operator. They wipe the lost
   second-factor credential from your account (your password is
   not affected). On next sign-in, Keycloak prompts you to set up
   2FA from scratch with your new device.

Same identity check as Case 2 — confirm out-of-band that it is
really you.

## Case 4: Multiple administrators are locked out at the same time

If everyone with admin access has lost something and no one can
sign in to your dashboard, your operator still has a separate
recovery path that does not depend on your email or your
dashboard. They can reset administrator credentials directly on
the running server. Contact them with the situation; they will
get an admin account back to you within ~30 minutes.

## What you can do today to prevent this

1. **Save your TOTP recovery codes** when you set up 2FA. Most
   authenticator apps offer a one-time download of recovery
   codes. Print them, put them in a safe.
2. **Use a password manager** that syncs across devices, with its
   own 2FA. Losing one phone does not lose all access.
3. **Run the [Disaster prevention checklist](disaster-prevention.md)**
   at least once a quarter. The "Export recovery secrets" button
   gives you an offline copy of your administrator credentials in
   one click.

## Notes

- The "I forgot my password" link only appears for accounts in
  the **vps** realm (regular users). Service accounts, robot
  accounts, and the Keycloak admin console itself do not show
  this option — for those, your operator is always the path.
- The reset link is single-use and expires after a short window.
  If you click it twice, the second click fails with "link
  expired" — start over with a new self-service request.
